\title[Sharp two-sided asymptotics for Euler's constant]
{Sharp two-sided summand asymptotics for the Euler--Mascheroni constant,
with a certified enclosure and a small-denominator obstruction}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{8 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a complete, effective two-sided theory of the classical approximants
$g_n = H_n - \log(n+1)$ to the Euler--Mascheroni constant $\gamma$, based on a single
elementary device: \emph{derivative certificates} for the logarithmic ratio
$\Lambda(z) = \log\frac{1+z}{1-z}$ on $[0,1)$, evaluated at $z = \frac{1}{2m+1}$.
Writing $t_k = g_{k+1}-g_k$, we prove the purely rational squeeze
$\frac{1}{2(k+2)^2} \le \frac{1}{2(k+1)(k+2)} \le t_k \le \frac{1}{2(k+1)^2}$; the sharp
two-sided tail estimate
$\frac{1}{2(n+1)} + \frac{1}{14(n+1)^2} \le \gamma - g_n \le \frac{1}{2(n+1)} + \frac{1}{12(n+1)^2}$
for all $n \ge 0$, whence $0 < \gamma - g_n \le \frac{1}{2n}$ for $n \ge 1$; and the two-sided
inverse-square bound $\frac{1}{14(n+1)^2} \le \gamma - a_n \le \frac{1}{12(n+1)^2}$ for the
midpoint-corrected approximants $a_n = g_n + \frac{1}{2(n+1)}$, so that their error is of order
exactly $n^{-2}$. Evaluating the tail estimate at $n = 15$, where
$g_{15} = \frac{1195757}{360360} - 4\log 2$, gives the certified enclosure
$0.5771692 < \gamma < 0.5772158$ of width $4.66\cdot 10^{-5}$, and a finite integer check then
yields a small-denominator obstruction: $\gamma \ne p/q$ for every integer $p$ and every
$1 \le q \le 148$, the threshold being optimal for this enclosure since $\frac{86}{149}$ lies
inside the interval. We also record an information-theoretic reading of the construction:
$t_k$ is the Kullback--Leibler divergence between consecutive exponential laws, the symmetrized
divergence satisfies $D(a\|b)+D(b\|a) = \frac{(a-b)^2}{ab}$, and this yields an exact
summability criterion for chains of positive rates with bounded ratios. Finally, we state the
Ap\'ery-style linear-forms criterion for irrationality and prove that the midpoint-accelerated
family provably cannot satisfy it, since $(n+1)^2(\gamma-a_n) \in [\tfrac1{14},\tfrac1{12}]$
never tends to zero.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Euler--Mascheroni constant, harmonic numbers, Euler--Maclaurin correction,
